Qt module:   qtmultimedia
Base tag:    v6.11.1
Applies to:  src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/playbackengine/qffmpegframe_p.h,
             src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/playbackengine/qffmpegstreamdecoder.cpp,
             src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/playbackengine/qffmpegsubtitlerenderer.cpp,
             src/multimedia/video/qvideoframe_p.h,
             src/multimedia/video/qvideoframe.h,
             src/multimedia/video/qvideoframe.cpp,
             src/multimedia/platform/qplatformvideosink_p.h,
             src/multimedia/platform/qplatformvideosink.cpp,
             src/multimedia/video/qvideosink.h,
             src/multimedia/video/qvideosink.cpp,
             src/multimediaquick/qsgvideonode_p.h,
             src/multimediaquick/qsgvideonode_p.cpp

Fixes two related bugs in one patch: (1) Vivace crashes outright when a
user selects an embedded bitmap-type subtitle track (DVD subtitles, PGS,
DVB -- anything image-based rather than text); (2) even past that crash,
Qt Multimedia's subtitle pipeline has no way to actually display a
bitmap-type subtitle at all, only text.

Why: reported against a real-world .mp4 file with an embedded
`dvd_subtitle`-codec subtitle track: selecting that track after seeking
into the file crashed Vivace every time. Diagnosed with real evidence:
`ffprobe` confirmed the subtitle codec is `dvd_subtitle` (bitmap-based,
not text); Windows Event Viewer showed several prior crash records for
`vivace.exe`, all with an identical fault offset inside
`ffmpegmediaplugin.dll` -- proof this is fully deterministic, not
environment-dependent; reproduced live under a debugger; then read Qt's
own source directly (not from memory) and found the exact bug:
`StreamDecoder::decodeSubtitle()`'s text-extraction loop checks
`if (r->text)`, and if that's null falls into an `else` branch that
unconditionally does `const char *ass = r->ass; ... while (*ass) { ... }`
-- but a **bitmap-type** `AVSubtitleRect` (`SUBTITLE_BITMAP`) populates
neither `text` NOR `ass` (bitmap rects use `data`/`w`/`h` instead), so
`while (*ass)` dereferences a null pointer unconditionally whenever a
rect is bitmap-type. Upstream Qt bug, not a Vivace bug, and not fixable
in Vivace's own compiled code -- the crash happens inside Qt's own
internal decode thread, a code path Vivace never calls directly, so there
is no call site in Vivace's own source where a guard could intercept it.
`QMediaMetaData` has no subtitle-codec key either, so Vivace has no
public-API way to detect "this track is bitmap-based" ahead of time to
refuse activation gracefully instead of letting it crash.

Per this project's working rule ("read the reference implementation
before implementing a feature"), SMPlayer's own source was checked before
going further -- it has nothing to port: SMPlayer's mpv backend decodes
and composites bitmap subtitles entirely inside the mpv process itself;
SMPlayer's own Qt/C++ code never touches subtitle pixels. So the
investigation continued into Qt Multimedia's own internal pipeline
instead, end to end:

  StreamDecoder::decodeSubtitle() (FFmpeg avcodec_decode_subtitle2) ->
  Frame -> SubtitleRenderer::renderInternal() -> QVideoSink::
  setSubtitleText() -> QPlatformVideoSink::setSubtitleText() (stored) ->
  QPlatformVideoSink::setVideoFrame() stamps the text onto every new
  QVideoFrame via frame.setSubtitleText() -> QSGVideoNode::
  updateSubtitle() (Qt Quick scene graph, private to Qt) reads
  frame.subtitleText() and composites a QSGInternalTextNode directly over
  the video.

This whole pipeline only carries TEXT. FFmpeg's AVSubtitleRect has a
SUBTITLE_BITMAP type (used by dvdsub/pgssub/dvbsub decoders) that
populates rects[i]->data[0] (a palette-index bitmap) + data[1] (an
nb_colors-entry RGBA palette) instead of text/ass. So the fix has to live
entirely inside Qt Multimedia's own subtitle pipeline, end to end, with
zero Vivace-side changes.

Fix: extend the pipeline above, in parallel with the existing text path,
to also carry an optional bitmap image:

- qffmpegstreamdecoder.cpp: decodeSubtitle() now branches on
  r->type == SUBTITLE_BITMAP, intercepting those rects before they would
  otherwise fall into the unconditional r->ass dereference (this is what
  fixes the crash). The palette-index bitmap is converted to a
  QImage::Format_ARGB32 image (each pixel = palette[index]; verified
  against FFmpeg's own dvdsubdec.c that its RGBA palette convention --
  b | (g<<8) | (r<<16) | (alpha<<24) -- is already the native in-memory
  layout QImage::Format_ARGB32 expects, so no byte-swapping is needed).
  The rect's placement (r->x/y/w/h) is in the subtitle decoder's own
  coded-frame space, so the AVCodecContext's width/height (set from the
  container's declared subtitle-plane resolution -- e.g. the DVD VOB's
  video size for dvdsub) is carried alongside it as imageCodedSize, so a
  later consumer can scale the placement into whatever it is actually
  displaying at. (Falls back to the rect's own extent when the codec
  context has no size at all, rather than dividing by zero downstream.)
  Only the first bitmap rect per event is converted (these formats
  normally emit exactly one).

- qffmpegframe_p.h, qffmpegsubtitlerenderer.cpp, qvideoframe(_p).h/.cpp,
  qplatformvideosink(_p).h/.cpp, qvideosink.h/.cpp: thread the
  (image, rect, codedSize) triple through Frame -> SubtitleRenderer ->
  QVideoSink::setSubtitleImage()/subtitleImage()/subtitleImageRect()/
  subtitleImageCodedSize() (a new public API, symmetric with the existing
  subtitleText()) -> QPlatformVideoSink (mutex-protected storage,
  mirroring the text member exactly) -> stamped onto every QVideoFrame via
  a new QVideoFrame::setSubtitleImage()/subtitleImage()/
  subtitleImageRect()/subtitleImageCodedSize(), symmetric with
  setSubtitleText()/subtitleText().

- qplatformvideosink.cpp: setVideoFrame() no longer stamps its own
  subtitle state onto an incoming frame that already carries one from an
  upstream sink while this sink has none of its own. Upstream stamps
  unconditionally, and because QVideoFrame is explicitly shared and
  setSubtitleText()/setSubtitleImage() write straight through d without
  detaching, that stamp does not merely fail to add a subtitle -- it
  ERASES the one another QVideoSink already put on that very frame.
  This is what made the bitmap subtitle render on Windows but not on
  Linux for the exact same patch: nothing platform-specific at all, just
  configuration. Vivace implements its negative A/V-delay offset by
  routing the player into an intercept QVideoSink and re-dispatching each
  frame into the VideoOutput's own sink a few ms later
  (PlayerController::applyVideoDelayRouting()/presentDueFrames()); the
  FFmpeg SubtitleRenderer stamps the subtitle onto the *intercept* sink,
  and the second setVideoFrame() call -- on the display sink, whose own
  subtitle state is empty -- then wiped it again before QSGVideoNode ever
  saw it. The Windows machine had no per-device audio delay configured,
  so it took the direct route and worked; the Linux machine had -300 ms
  configured for its output device, so it took the intercept route and
  rendered nothing. Diagnosed by instrumenting decodeSubtitle(),
  SubtitleRenderer, QPlatformVideoSink and
  QSGVideoNode::updateSubtitleImage() with qWarning() and reading the
  result back from vivace.log: the decode side produced correct images
  (e.g. 264x56 at (227,388) in a 720x480 coded frame) and the sink stored
  them, yet every frame reaching QSGVideoNode had a null subtitleImage()
  -- and clearing the configured device delay made it appear immediately.
  This bug is NOT specific to the bitmap path: embedded *text* subtitles
  are silently dropped through the same re-dispatch in exactly the same
  way on stock Qt, which the same instrumented run confirmed
  (subtitleText() arrived empty at the node with the delay configured,
  populated without it). Hence the guard covers both members.

- qsgvideonode_p.h/.cpp: QSGVideoNode gains a second child node,
  m_subtitleImageNode (a QSGImageNode, created via
  sceneGraphContext()->createImageNode() + a QSGTexture from
  sceneGraphRenderContext()->createTexture(image) -- the same scene-graph
  primitives QSGInternalTextNode itself is built from, just the plain
  image-node flavor instead of a text-layout one), composited alongside
  the existing text node. updateSubtitleImage() mirrors updateSubtitle()'s
  own shape exactly (compute desired state, early-return if nothing
  changed -- this idempotency check is what keeps
  updateSubtitleImage()'s own call to setSubtitleGeometry() at the end
  from recursing, exactly as updateSubtitle()'s pre-existing call already
  relied on m_subtitleLayout.update()'s own change-detection to do the
  same for the text node). imageRect/imageCodedSize are scaled into the
  same local, pre-rotation "subtitleFrameSize" coordinate space
  updateSubtitle()'s text layout already uses, so the two nodes share one
  extracted subtitleTransform() (translate+rotate) helper for final
  placement -- refactored out of the previously-inline body of
  setSubtitleGeometry(), behavior unchanged for the text node.
  QSGImageNode itself has no setMatrix() (unlike QSGInternalTextNode,
  which is-a QSGTransformNode) -- a real build caught this (MSVC C2039,
  "'setMatrix': is not a member of 'QSGImageNode'"), since it's not
  something visible just from reading the header. Fixed by wrapping the
  image node in a plain QSGTransformNode (m_subtitleImageTransformNode),
  appended as the image node's parent instead of appending the image node
  directly to QSGVideoNode; subtitleTransform()'s matrix is set on that
  wrapper instead of on the image node.

To apply: cd <qtmultimedia source root> && git apply
patches/qtmultimedia-subtitle-bitmap.patch (or `patch -p1 < ...patch` if
not a git checkout), then rebuild qtmultimedia (or all of Qt) and Vivace.
No Vivace-side (this repo's own src/qml) changes are needed or included
-- Qt already stamps QVideoFrame::subtitleText() onto frames and
composites it with no Vivace code involved; this patch adds the bitmap
equivalent alongside it the same way.

VERIFIED (2026-08-06, Windows): compiles cleanly (a real `cmake --build`
of qtmultimedia caught and led to fixing the QSGImageNode::setMatrix()
issue described above -- QSGImageNode has no matrix of its own, fixed by
wrapping it in a QSGTransformNode) and confirmed working end to end
against a real dvd_subtitle-codec track: no crash, the bitmap subtitle
renders correctly positioned, updates across multiple dialogue/caption
lines as playback continues, and stays correctly positioned/scaled after
resizing the window (maximized) mid-playback.

VERIFIED (2026-08-06, Linux/Ubuntu, RHI backend OpenGL under Wayland):
with the qplatformvideosink.cpp guard above added, confirmed rendering
end to end against the same dvd_subtitle-codec track in all four
combinations -- bitmap and text subtitles, each with the A/V-delay
intercept route active (-300 ms configured for the audio device) and
with the direct route. Checked in actual composited pixels, not just in
logs: a QQuickWindow::grabWindow() harness reproducing Vivace's
intercept-and-re-dispatch routing showed the subtitle bitmap drawn
bottom-aligned over the video with correct scaling and correct alpha (no
opaque black box behind the glyphs -- QSGRenderContext::createTexture()
already defaults to CreateTexture_Alpha, and QSGPlainTexture converts
Format_ARGB32 to RGBA8888_Premultiplied on upload, so no premultiply
handling is needed on our side). Vivace itself was then re-run with the
-300 ms delay still configured and confirmed to build one image node per
dialogue line. The guard is a pure widening of when the frame's existing
subtitle survives, so the already-verified Windows behavior (direct
route, sink state always authoritative) is unchanged.

RE-VERIFIED (2026-08-06, Windows, after the qplatformvideosink.cpp guard
above was added): rebuilt qtmultimedia and Vivace against the updated
patch and re-ran all three cases end to end, interactively, against real
media: (1) direct route (no per-device audio delay configured) with a
dvd_subtitle-codec track -- still renders correctly, confirming no
regression on the case already verified before this guard existed; (2)
delay route (-300 ms configured for the active output device) with the
same dvd_subtitle-codec track -- renders correctly, the case that was
silently broken on Windows too before this fix, just never noticed
because Windows testing up to this point happened to have no per-device
delay configured; (3) delay route (-300 ms) with an embedded mov_text
track -- renders correctly, confirming the text-subtitle half of the fix
(QSGInternalTextNode's path, not just the new bitmap path) also works on
Windows. Reset the delay back to 0 afterward.

FOLLOW-UP (2026-08-16): bitmap-subtitle edge smoothing added, matching a
user report against the same real-world .mp4 file this patch was
originally written for -- its dvd_subtitle-codec track looked "jaggy" for
the same reason DVD-disc subtitles do (see Vivace's own
PlayerController::softenSubtitleEdges(), added the same day for the
DVD-disc case, whose comment explains the underlying cause and the
triangular-kernel-not-box-blur choice in full). Since this pipeline is
entirely inside qtmultimedia with no way to call back into Vivace,
qffmpegstreamdecoder.cpp's decodeSubtitle() gained its OWN copy of the
same blur algorithm (vivaceSoftenBitmapSubtitle(), a static function
right above StreamDecoder::StreamDecoder()), applied to the decoded
QImage immediately after it's built from the palette bitmap, before it's
threaded any further through the pipeline described above -- so every
downstream consumer (Frame, SubtitleRenderer, QVideoSink, QSGVideoNode)
is unaffected and needs no changes. Controlled by the
VIVACE_SUBTITLE_BITMAP_SMOOTHING environment variable (0 = off, 1-3 =
increasing radius, read fresh via qEnvironmentVariableIntValue() on every
call rather than cached, so it can change without restarting) rather
than a compile-time constant, specifically so Vivace's existing
Preferences > Subtitles > DVD subtitles > Smoothing control (a QML/C++
Settings value with no way to reach into this patched Qt code directly)
can still govern it: PlayerController::setDvdSubtitleSmoothing() (Vivace
side, src/playercontroller.cpp) now also calls qputenv() with the same
value whenever it changes, so ONE Preferences control transparently
governs both the DVD-disc pipeline (Vivace's own code) and this
embedded-file pipeline (this patch). NOT YET independently re-verified
against a real rebuild in this environment -- same limitation as every
other change to this patch; needs the user to rebuild qtmultimedia and
Vivace and confirm the bitmap subtitle in the reported .mp4 file now
looks smoother with Smoothing > 0 and unchanged at Smoothing = 0.


INVESTIGATED 2026-08-17, NOT FIXED (real Blu-ray disc testing, PGS
subtitle tracks): selecting a Blu-ray subtitle track (hdmv_pgs_subtitle
codec) via Subtitles > Track correctly updates the active-track
selection/checkmark (confirmed: Vivace's own selection UI and
activeSubtitleTrack mechanism work correctly for Blu-ray, same as any
ordinary file), but never produces a visible subtitle at all, even at
real, confirmed subtitle-packet timestamps (verified directly via
ffprobe against the real disc's raw .m2ts -- genuine PGS packets with
non-trivial rect data exist at ~9s/~32s/~40s and beyond).

Root cause identified: this function's `if (end <= start) { ...; return;
}` check rejects every real PGS subtitle event unconditionally, because
FFmpeg's own hdmv_pgs_subtitle decoder leaves AVSubtitle::
end_display_time at 0 (PGS doesn't encode an explicit per-event
duration; it relies on a LATER, separate empty "clear" segment instead)
-- so subtitle.pts's start/end always collapse to the same value and the
bitmap-decode loop just below this check (added by the base version of
this patch) is never reached for Blu-ray content at all.

A fix was attempted: give a zero/negative-duration event with real rect
content (num_rects > 0) a several-second fallback duration instead of
discarding it, so it can reach the existing bitmap-decode loop. This
DID let the code reach that loop for the first time ever with real PGS
data -- but it also produced a real, reproducible crash on the exact
same real disc used to test it: a Debug CRT heap-validation failure
(_CrtIsValidHeapPointer, debug_heap.cpp:904), confirmed via cdb attached
to the running process. Investigation notes, so a future attempt
doesn't have to re-derive them:
  - The crash is NOT the `avsubtitle_free()` call an earlier version of
    this attempt also added in the num_rects==0 branch -- removing that
    call alone did not stop the crash on a retest against the same disc.
  - The crash correlates with a `QObject: Cannot create children for a
    parent that is in a different thread` warning naming Vivace's own
    PlayerController as the parent, appearing (per vivace.log) only
    once this fix lets a real PGS Frame flow through -- never in any
    earlier, successful Blu-ray session (checked directly: earlier
    preserved vivace.log captures from before this fix have zero
    occurrences of this warning or of any heap-corruption message).
  - The bitmap-decode loop itself (qsgvideonode_p.cpp's
    updateSubtitleImage(), reviewed directly) doesn't obviously explain
    the warning -- QSGImageNode/QSGTransformNode/QSGTexture are QSGNode-
    derived, not QObject-derived, so they shouldn't be able to trigger a
    QObject cross-thread-parenting warning by themselves. The actual
    trigger (something that constructs a real QObject parented to
    PlayerController from Qt Multimedia's own worker thread, apparently
    only once a subtitle-track StreamDecoder/Renderer actually processes
    a real Frame rather than always hitting an early return) was not
    pinned down further -- live debugging in this environment proved
    unreliable past this point (a Retry-button click meant to let cdb
    catch a real breakpoint blocked indefinitely via SendMessage rather
    than resolving).
  - FFmpeg's real pgssubdec.c source (fetched directly, n7.1 tag) was
    checked and RULED OUT two specific hypotheses: the palette buffer is
    always allocated at the full AVPALETTE_SIZE (256 entries) regardless
    of the real color count, same as DVD's decoder, so an out-of-bounds
    palette read isn't the cause; linesize[0] is set equal to the
    rect's own width with no padding, so an out-of-bounds bitmap-row
    read isn't the cause either.

Given the crash is worse than the original "silently doesn't render"
symptom, the fix attempt was REVERTED -- this function is back to its
original, base-patch behavior (unconditionally rejecting any
zero/negative-duration subtitle event). Blu-ray subtitle track selection
itself works correctly and is safe to use (confirmed no crash with a
real subtitle track selected and real content played through it); the
actual bitmap/text never appears on screen for Blu-ray (PGS) content
specifically. DVD/embedded-file bitmap subtitles are unaffected --
they don't hit this same codec-specific quirk and were not touched by
either the attempted fix or this revert.
